 "charbel-v2-story-data.js": {
  "A Boy in the High Mountains": 1,
  "In a small village called Bekaa Kafra, high in the mountains of Lebanon, a boy named Youssef Antoun Makhlouf was born on May 8, 1828. His home was a quiet place of snow, cedar trees, and little stone houses, where families prayed together morning and night. When Youssef was only three years old, his father died. His mother Brigitta raised him with deep faith, and monks in his own family showed him what a life given to God could look like. Even as a small boy, Youssef learned that God stays close in happy days and in sad ones.": 1,
  "Birth date, village, family context, and the early loss of his father are documented in biographical records and recalled at his beatification.": 1,
  "The Shepherd Boy's Secret Place": 1,
  "Like many boys in Bekaa Kafra, Youssef helped his family by watching their cow out in the fields. The people of his village still tell how he would slip away to a little grotto among the rocks, a quiet cave where he could be alone with God. There he knelt and prayed, far from noise and hurry. It is a story handed down with love by his own villagers, not a scene anyone wrote down at the time. But it shows what Youssef loved most. Even as a boy, he was learning to listen to God in silence.": 1,
  "The shepherd boy's grotto prayer is a village tradition handed down in Bekaa Kafra and Annaya devotion, not a scene recorded at the time.": 1,
  "A Brave Yes": 1,
  "When Youssef was twenty-three years old, he made a brave choice. In 1851 he said goodbye to his mother and his village, and he walked to the monastery of Our Lady of Mayfouk to become a monk. Before long he moved to the monastery of Saint Maron in Annaya, where he received a new name: Charbel. In 1853 he made his solemn vows, promising a life of prayer, poverty, and obedience. Maronite biographies tell us his mother visited him and blessed him, praying that God would make her son a saint.": 1,
  "The Vatican canonization homily gives the formation path: Mayfouk, Annaya, and vows; the mother's blessing is recounted in Maronite biographies.": 1,
  "Learning with a Holy Teacher": 1,
  "After his vows, Charbel was sent to study at the monastery of Saints Cyprian and Justina in Kfifan. His teacher there was a wise and humble monk named Nimatullah al-Hardini, who would one day become a saint himself. Charbel studied Scripture, the liturgy, and the teachings of the Church with a quiet and listening heart. He was known as a student who worked hard, obeyed cheerfully, and spoke little. A holy teacher was helping to shape a holy student.": 1,
  "Kfifan studies are in the Vatican formation account; Saint Nimatullah al-Hardini as his teacher is documented in biographical records.": 1,
  "Father Charbel": 1,
  "On July 23, 1859, Charbel was ordained a priest, and he returned to Annaya as Father Charbel. For sixteen years he shared the daily life of his brother monks: their work in the fields, their simple meals, and their long hours of prayer. He rose in the dark for the first prayers of the day, and he gave his whole heart to the holy Mass. His brothers saw a man who was always obedient, always gentle, and always close to God. The hidden years were making him ready.": 1,
  "Ordination on July 23, 1859 and sixteen years of community life are stated in the Vatican canonization homily.": 1,
  "The Little Hermitage": 1,
  "In February 1875, Father Charbel received permission for something he had desired for a long time. He moved up the mountain to a tiny hermitage named for Saints Peter and Paul, where a few hermits lived in silence and simplicity. There he prayed deep into the night, fasted, and worked with his hands in the fields. He owned almost nothing, and he asked for nothing. For twenty-three years, that little hermitage was his whole world, and God was his whole life.": 1,
  "The move to the hermitage of Saints Peter and Paul in February 1875 and twenty-three years there are in the Vatican homily and monastic records.": 1,
  "A Story They Tell: The Lamp": 1,
  "The monks of Annaya lovingly tell a story from those quiet years. One night, they say, someone playfully filled Father Charbel's lamp with water instead of oil, and the lamp went on shining all the same. This is a tradition, a story handed down with love, not one of the miracles the Church examined when it made him a saint. Still, it tells us something true about how his brothers saw him. They believed God was near to this gentle man, and his light did not go out.": 1,
  "This page presents a monastic tradition account, not a formal miracle used as canonical proof by the Church.": 1,
  "His Last Mass": 1,
  "On December 16, 1898, Father Charbel was celebrating the holy Mass when he was struck by a sudden illness. His brother monks carried him to his cell, and for eight days he lay quietly, still whispering the prayers he loved. On Christmas Eve, December 24, he gave his soul to God, on the very night the world waits for Jesus to be born. He was seventy years old, and he had spent his whole life getting ready for that holy night. The next day, through the falling snow, the monks carried him to his rest.": 1,
  "Illness during Mass on December 16, 1898 and death on December 24, 1898 are documented in monastic and biographical records.": 1,
  "A Light for the Whole World": 1,
  "After his death, wonderful things began to be reported. People saw lights shining over his tomb at Annaya, and pilgrims came from near and far to pray. Many sick people said they were healed after asking Father Charbel to pray for them, and the Church studied these reports slowly and carefully. On December 5, 1965, Pope Paul VI declared him Blessed. On October 9, 1977, before great crowds in Rome, he was declared a saint. The boy from a mountain village had become a saint for the whole world.": 1,
  "Beatification (Dec 5, 1965) and canonization (Oct 9, 1977) are official Church acts by Pope Paul VI; tomb lights and healings are reported, studied carefully.": 1,
  "Points of reflection": 1,
  "First, Charbel listened. He made room for God in silence, and we can do the same with a few quiet minutes each day. When could you be still and listen this week?\n\nSecond, he trusted. He lost his father when he was small, but he did not give up on God. When something makes you sad, who can you talk to, and what prayer can you pray?\n\nThird, he was faithful in small things. Watching a cow, working in a field, and washing dishes can all become ways to love God. What small job of yours could become a prayer?\n\nFourth, he stayed close to Jesus in the holy Mass, and everything he did began there. Choose one small step this week, and ask Saint Charbel to pray for you as you take it.": 1,
  "An original reflection applying the Gospel's call to listen, trust, and serve to Saint Charbel's documented life; not a recorded speech.": 1
 },
